    <briefdescription>
<para>A dictionary for key - value pairs. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This dictionary object must be used in the workflow to configure other objects in the training process.</para>
<para>A <ref refid="classConfig" kindref="compound">Config</ref> object stores information such as:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>cutoff distances</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>descriptor parameters</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>paths to training database files</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>and many more...</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>Every object using a <ref refid="classConfig" kindref="compound">Config</ref> class specifies a list of required and optional KEY - VALUE pairs.</para>
<para>The config keys and values are case insensitive.</para>
<para><hruler/>
 <heading level="1">List of supported key-value pairs:</heading>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1DBFILE"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>DBFILE</bold> &lt;string&gt; str</para>
<para>Absolute or relative path to the training database file. The relative path is to the script working directory. More than one can be specified to include several datasets.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>DBFILE /home/user12/project/db.dat</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1INIT2B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>INIT2B</bold> &lt;string&gt; true/false</para>
<para>Whether to calculate two-body type (<ref refid="classD2__Base" kindref="compound">D2_Base</ref>) descriptor.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>INIT2B true</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1INIT3B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>INIT3B</bold> &lt;string&gt; true/false</para>
<para>Whether to calculate three-body type (<ref refid="classD3__Base" kindref="compound">D3_Base</ref>) descriptor.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>INIT3B true</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1INITMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>INITMB</bold> &lt;string&gt; true/false</para>
<para>Whether to calculate many-body type (<ref refid="classDM__Base" kindref="compound">DM_Base</ref>) descriptor.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>INITMB true</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1TYPE2B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>TYPE2B</bold> &lt;string&gt; name</para>
<para>Type of a two-body descriptor to be used. Any type which inherits from <ref refid="classD2__Base" kindref="compound">D2_Base</ref> is fine here.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>TYPE2B <ref refid="classD2__LJ" kindref="compound">D2_LJ</ref></computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1TYPE3B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>TYPE3B</bold> &lt;string&gt; name</para>
<para>Type of a three-body descriptor to be used. Any type which inherits from <ref refid="classD3__Base" kindref="compound">D3_Base</ref> is fine here.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>TYPE3B D3_LJ</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1TYPEMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>TYPEMB</bold> &lt;string&gt; name</para>
<para>Type of a many-body descriptor to be used. Any type which inherits from <ref refid="classDM__Base" kindref="compound">DM_Base</ref> is fine here.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>TYPEMB <ref refid="classDM__EAM" kindref="compound">DM_EAM</ref></computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1RCTYPE2B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>RCTYPE2B</bold> &lt;string&gt; name</para>
<para>Cutoff type to be used with two-body descriptor. Any type which inherits from CutoffBase is fine here.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>RCTYPE2B BPCutoff</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1RCTYPE3B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>RCTYPE3B</bold> &lt;string&gt; name</para>
<para>Cutoff type to be used with three-body descriptor. Any type which inherits from CutoffBase is fine here.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>RCTYPE3B BPCutoff</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1RCTYPEMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>RCTYPEMB</bold> &lt;string&gt; name</para>
<para>Cutoff type to be used with many-body descriptor. Any type which inherits from CutoffBase is fine here.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>RCTYPEMB BPCutoff</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1RCUT2B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>RCUT2B</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Cutoff distance used by the two-body type descriptor.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>RCUT2B 6.7</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1RCUT3B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>RCUT3B</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Cutoff distance used by the three-body type descriptor.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>RCUT3B 5.5</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1RCUTMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>RCUTMB</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Cutoff distance used by the many-body type descriptor.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>RCUTMB 8.5</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1AGRID3B"/><anchor id="classConfig_1AGRIDMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>AGRID3B</bold>&lt;int&gt; N</para>
<para><bold>AGRIDMB</bold>&lt;int&gt; N</para>
<para>Size of angular grid for three- and some many-body types.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1CGRID2B"/><anchor id="classConfig_1CGRID3B"/><anchor id="classConfig_1CGRIDMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>CGRID2B</bold> <bold>CGRID3B</bold> <bold>CGRIDMB</bold></para>
<para>A number of position parameters of the radial basis functions for a two-, three- and many-body descriptor, e.g. position of a Gaussian function. The parameter list can be provided or generated automatically. Note that not all descriptors use this parameter, e.g. <ref refid="classD2__LJ" kindref="compound">D2_LJ</ref> has fixed grid.<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>CGRID2B</bold> &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para>Provide a list of position parameters. Note that N1 value can be negative in principle but NOT NEGATIVE INTEGER (e.g. -1 or -1.000 won&apos;t work, but -1.0001 is fine).</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>CGRID2B 1.14 3.45 4.55 6.73</computeroutput></para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>CGRID2B</bold> &lt;int&gt; -A N &lt;double&gt; min max</para>
<para>Generate a series of <computeroutput>N</computeroutput> points between <computeroutput>min</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>max</computeroutput> with <computeroutput>-A</computeroutput> algorithm. <computeroutput>min&gt;=0</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>max&gt;=0</computeroutput>. Available algorithms: &apos;-1&apos; evenly spaced points between <computeroutput>min</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>max</computeroutput>. &apos;-2&apos; as above but numbers are evenly spaced on a log scale.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>CGRID2B -1 6 0.5 7.4</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SGRID2B"/><anchor id="classConfig_1SGRID3B"/><anchor id="classConfig_1SGRIDMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>SGRID2B</bold> <bold>SGRID3B</bold> <bold>SGRIDMB</bold></para>
<para>A number of shape parameters of the radial basis functions for a two-, three- and many-body descriptor, e.g. width of a Gaussian function. The parameter list can be provided or generated automatically.<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>SGRID2B</bold> &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para>Provide a set of shape parameters. All numbers must be positive. e.g. <computeroutput>SGRID2B 0.12 0.33 1.0 5.1</computeroutput></para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>SGRID2B</bold> &lt;int&gt; -A N &lt;double&gt; min max</para>
<para>Generate a series of <computeroutput>N</computeroutput> points between <computeroutput>min</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>max</computeroutput> with <computeroutput>-A</computeroutput> algorithm. <computeroutput>min&gt;=0</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>max&gt;=0</computeroutput>. Available algorithms: &apos;-1&apos; evenly spaced points between <computeroutput>min</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>max</computeroutput>. &apos;-2&apos; as above but numbers are evenly spaced on a log scale.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>SGRID2B -1 6 0.5 7.4</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1MPARAMS"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>MPARAMS</bold> &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para>List of parameters used by some models. See model description for more details. Note that many models do not require this parameter at all.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>MPARAMS 0.1</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1FORCE"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>FORCE</bold> &lt;string&gt; true/false</para>
<para>Whether to calculate FD and use forces in a training,</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>FORCE true</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1STRESS"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>STRESS</bold> &lt;string&gt; true/false</para>
<para>Whether to calculate SD and use cell stresses in a training,</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>STRESS false</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SBASIS"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>SBASIS</bold> &lt;int&gt; N</para>
<para>The number of basis functions to use with when constructing <ref refid="classDesignMatrix" kindref="compound">DesignMatrix</ref>.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>SBASIS 10</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1NORM"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>NORM</bold> &lt;string&gt; true/false</para>
<para>Set true to normalise descriptors.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>NORM true</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1LAMBDA"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>LAMBDA</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Default: <bold>LAMBDA</bold> -1</para>
<para>Regularisation parameter for BLR and KRR.<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>LAMBDA</bold> == 0 -&gt; use OLS</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>LAMBDA</bold> &gt; 0 -&gt; manually set to given value</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>LAMBDA</bold> &lt; 0 -&gt; use evidence-approximation to estimate <formula id="0">$\lambda$</formula></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>LAMBDA 1e-5</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1ALPHA"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>ALPHA</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Weight precision hyper-parameter. Starting guess used in evidence approximation algorithm. Default: ALPHA 1.0</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>ALPHA 0.23</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1BETA"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>BETA</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Noise precision hyper-parameter. Starting guess used in evidence approximation algorithm. Default: BETA 1.0</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>BETA 0.0001</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SETFL"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>SETFL</bold> &lt;string&gt; path/to/setfl/file</para>
<para>Path to setfl file with eam potentiaal</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>SETFL Ta1_Ravelo_2013.eam.alloy</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1BIAS"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>BIAS</bold> &lt;string&gt; true/false</para>
<para>Controls whether to append 1 to every descriptor. Increases DSIZE by 1.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>BIAS true</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1VERBOSE"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>VERBOSE</bold> &lt;int&gt; N</para>
<para>Provides detailed output for diagnostic purposes for N&gt;0. Default: VERBOSE 1</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>VERBOSE 0</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1EWEIGHT"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>EWEIGHT</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Global energy scalling factor for all configurations used in training. Note that energies are always scalled by 1/number of atoms. Individual scalling factors for every configuration can be set in a dataset file.</para>
<para>The combined scalling factor is: EWEIGHT*(configuration eweight)/(number of atoms)</para>
<para>Default: EWEIGHT 1.0</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>EWEIGHT 0.95</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1FWEIGHT"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>FWEIGHT</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Global force scalling factor for all configurations used in training. Note that each force component is always scalled by 1/(number of atoms)/3. Individual scalling factors for every configuration can be set in a dataset file.</para>
<para>The combined scalling factor is: FWEIGHT*(configuration fweight)/(number of atoms)/3</para>
<para>Default: FWEIGHT 1.0</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>FWEIGHT 0.1</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SWEIGHT"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>SWEIGHT</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Global stress scalling factor for all configurations used in training. Note that each stress component is always scalled by 1/6. Individual scalling factors for every configuration can be set in a dataset file.</para>
<para>The combined scalling factor is: SWEIGHT*(configuration Sweight)/6</para>
<para>Default: SWEIGHT 1.0</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>SWEIGHT 0.05</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1WEIGHTS"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>WEIGHTS</bold> &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para>WEIGHTS are machine learned coefficiens. This key is used during prediction for the lammps interface.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>WEIGHTS 0.12 1.2 0.3</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SIGMA"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>SIGMA</bold> &lt;int&gt; N &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para>Sigma matrix for Bayesian Linear Regression. Sigma is NxN matrix. Stored in col by col order.</para>
<para>1.2 2.3 2.2 3.3</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>Sigma 2 1.2 2.2 2.3 3.3</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1MODEL"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>MODEL</bold> &lt;string&gt; &lt;Model type&gt; &lt;Basis function or kernel type&gt;</para>
<para>MODEL Any class which inherits from Model class.</para>
<para>e.g. <computeroutput>MODEL BLR Linear</computeroutput> e.g. <computeroutput>MODEL BLR Polynomial2</computeroutput> e.g. <computeroutput>MODEL KRR LinearKernel</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1NSTDEV"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>NSTDEV</bold> &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para>NSTDEV <ref refid="classNormaliser" kindref="compound">Normaliser</ref> standard deviations.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1NMEAN"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>NMEAN</bold> &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para>NMEAN <ref refid="classNormaliser" kindref="compound">Normaliser</ref> means.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1BASIS"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>BASIS</bold> &lt;double&gt; N1 N2 N3 ...</para>
<para><ref refid="classBasis" kindref="compound">Basis</ref> vectors used by non-linear KRR model.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><hruler/>
 <heading level="1">List of internally used keys:</heading>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="warning"><para><anchor id="classConfig_1INTERNAL_KEY"/> <bold>INTERNAL_KEY</bold><linebreak/>
 Do not try to set internal keys. Corresponding values are calculated by the library when required. User can access internal key-value pairs as usually.</para>
</simplesect>
<anchor id="classConfig_1DSIZE"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>&lt;<ref refid="classConfig_1INTERNAL_KEY" kindref="member">INTERNAL_KEY</ref>&gt; <bold>DSIZE</bold> &lt;int&gt;</para>
<para>Total dimension of a descriptor. DSIZE = SIZE2B + SIZE3B + SIZEMB. Set by a Calculator.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SIZE2B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>&lt;<ref refid="classConfig_1INTERNAL_KEY" kindref="member">INTERNAL_KEY</ref>&gt; <bold>SIZE2B</bold> &lt;int&gt;</para>
<para>Dimension of a two-body descriptor. Set by a Calculator.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SIZE3B"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>&lt;<ref refid="classConfig_1INTERNAL_KEY" kindref="member">INTERNAL_KEY</ref>&gt; <bold>SIZE3B</bold> &lt;int&gt;</para>
<para>Dimension of a three-body descriptor. Set by a Calculator.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SIZEMB"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>&lt;<ref refid="classConfig_1INTERNAL_KEY" kindref="member">INTERNAL_KEY</ref>&gt; <bold>SIZEMB</bold> &lt;int&gt;</para>
<para>Dimension of a many-body descriptor. Set by a Calculator.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1ATOMS"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>ATOMS</bold> &lt;string&gt; A1 A2 ...</para>
<para>List of atom types. Read from training database files. e.g. <computeroutput>ATOMS Ti Nb</computeroutput></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1RCUTMAX"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>RCUTMAX</bold> &lt;string&gt; A1 A2 ...</para>
<para>Maximum cutoff distance, max(RCUT2B,RCUT3B,RCUTMB)</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1ESTDEV"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>ESTDEV</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Energy standard deviation (energy/atom).</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1FSTDEV"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>FSTDEV</bold> &lt;double&gt; N</para>
<para>Force standard deviation (energy/distance).</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><anchor id="classConfig_1SSTDEV"/><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>SSTDEV</bold> &lt;double&gt; xx xy xz yy yz zz</para>
<para>Stress standard deviations (energy units). </para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
